THREE RETAINED EDITS in src/game/world/gauntworld.c (commits 80622ab72, 1cb94fb11, fb5efccae).

(1) sdata2 EXTERN SIZES - a TU-wide addressing-mode defect, three call sites. Target `li r4,0 @lbl_80346F20(EMB_SDA21)`; ours `lis r4,...(ADDR16_HA)` + `addi r4,r4,...(ADDR16_LO)`. MWCC only small-data-addresses an extern whose DECLARED size is within the sdata threshold, and an unsized `[]` array is never eligible:
-extern char  lbl_80346F20[];   /* "TREAS_GOLD" (sdata2 copy) */
-extern char  lbl_80346F28[];   /* "TREAS_SILVER" (sdata2 copy) */
-extern char  lbl_80346F34[];   /* "%s_D" */
+extern const char lbl_80346F20[8];
+extern const char lbl_80346F28[8];
+extern const char lbl_80346F34[5];
The sizes are NOT guesses and NOT fudges: config/GUNE5D/symbols.txt records `size:0x8`, `size:0x8`, `size:0x5` for the three labels, and the old source comments ("TREAS_GOLD", "TREAS_SILVER") were simply wrong about the strings. Measured ladder proving the mechanism is the THRESHOLD and not const-ness: `[11]` = NEUTRAL-IDENTICAL (over threshold), unsized `const []` = REGRESSED back to lis (no size), `[8]` = the target's `li`. real 527 -> 524, fuzzy 94.1794 -> 95.2177.

(2) THE RECORD SUB-POINTER, four search loops. Target computes `addi r25,rREC,4` once per iteration into a callee-saved register and addresses BOTH `*(s32*)(rec+4)` and `(char*)(rec+0x28)` through it (`addi r4,r25,36`); ours folded everything onto one base:
     for (k = 0; k < n; k++) {
+        rsub = (s32*)(rec + 4);
-        if (strcmp(lbl_80346F10, (char*)(rec + 0x28)) == 0 &&
-            *(s32*)rec == 1 && *(s32*)(rec + 4) == 3) {
+        if (strcmp(lbl_80346F10, (char*)rsub + 0x24) == 0 &&
+            *(s32*)rec == 1 && *rsub == 3) {
             goto found_chicken;
         }
         rec += 0x50;
     }
(plus `s32* rsub;` in the declaration block; the same shape applied to found_apple, found_gold and found_silver). This is idiomatic for the function, which already declares `s32* sub = (s32*)((u8*)info + 4);` over the parallel iteminfo record. All four `addi` rows closed; multiset 22t -> 18t, unpaired 28 -> 10.

(3) THE CASE-10 SUBTYPE GUARD IS A SWITCH, NOT AN `||` CHAIN. Target at +0x5dc: `cmpwi 42 / beq accept / bge hi / cmpwi 41 / bge reject / b accept / cmpwi 44 / bge accept / b reject` - a binary range dispatch rejecting exactly {41,43}. Ours emitted two equality tests:
-    case 10:
-        if (*sub == 0x29 || *sub == 0x2B) {
-            break;
-        }
-        <body>
-        break;
+    case 10:
+        switch (*sub) {
+        case 0x29:
+        case 0x2B:
+            break;
+        default:
+            <body>
+            break;
+        }
+        break;
The inner-switch `break` exits the inner switch and falls onto the outer `break`, so the semantics are unchanged. multiset 18t -> 11t, count distance 10 -> 5, and every bge/cmpwi row vanished.

MEASURED NEGATIVE, do not retry: a SCOPED `#pragma peephole off` / `#pragma peephole on` pair wrapped around this function alone, to test whether the target's unfused `beq ->next; b ->far` guard pairs are the peephole-OFF tell that claim.law.per-lib-pragma-and-flag-map names. It is not. real 627 -> 683, insns 491 -> 505 against a target of 496, multiset 11t -> 61t, with 18 `mr` moves flipping to `addi` and the prologue restructuring. The TU is peephole-ON and correct; the branch pairs have another cause.
